.\" megapdf-cli(1): the manual page for MegaPDF's command-line extractor (#142, #395).
.\" Plain ASCII throughout: groff -man without -K reads its input as Latin-1 and warns
.\" on every byte of a UTF-8 dash, and lintian runs it exactly that way.
.\"
.\" The OPTIONS and EXIT STATUS sections are the --help text and the exit-code list in
.\" core/cli/megapdf_cli.cpp (PrintUsage), option for option and code for code. When an
.\" option is added or an exit code changes there, change it here in the same commit:
.\" nothing generates this file, and a page that disagrees with --help is worse than
.\" no page.
.\"
.\" Shipped by tools/build-linux-app.sh (share/man/man1/ in the tree), installed by
.\" tools/linux/install.sh ($PREFIX/share/man/man1/) and tools/linux/build-deb.sh
.\" (/usr/share/man/man1/megapdf-cli.1.gz). Check it with `man -l tools/linux/megapdf-cli.1`
.\" and `groff -man -z -ww tools/linux/megapdf-cli.1` (or `mandoc -Tlint`).
.TH MEGAPDF-CLI 1 "2026-09-26" "MegaPDF 2.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
megapdf-cli \- extract text or Markdown from a PDF, from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B megapdf-cli extract
.I file.pdf
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B megapdf-cli \-\-version
.br
.B megapdf-cli \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B megapdf-cli
reads a PDF and writes its text, in reading order, to standard output or to a file.
It is the same engine MegaPDF itself uses (the shared core over MegaPDF's own patched
PDFium), packaged as a standalone program: it opens no window, needs no display and
no desktop session, makes no network connection of any kind, and runs the same way in
a terminal, a script, a cron job or a container.
.PP
The one command is
.BR extract .
By default it writes every page as plain text: paragraphs are joined into blocks, the
running headers, footers and page numbers a document repeats on every page are
dropped, filled-in form fields are included, and a form feed separates one page from
the next. Each of those defaults has an option below.
.PP
With
.B \-\-format md
the output is Markdown built from the document's inferred structure rather than a
flat dump of its words: headings, paragraphs, lists, form fields and (in a tagged PDF)
tables are written as Markdown headings, paragraphs, lists, definition lines and pipe
tables. Where the PDF carries a structure tree (a tagged PDF), that is what is read;
where it does not, or with
.BR \-\-heuristic ,
the structure is inferred from the layout of the page.
.PP
A page with no text layer (a scan, a photograph of a page) yields the placeholder
.B [Page N has no text layer]
in the output and, unless
.B \-\-quiet
is given, a note on standard error. MegaPDF does not do OCR.
.PP
A password is never taken from the command line, where every process on the machine
could read it. It comes from the first line of a file
.RB ( \-\-password\-file )
or of standard input
.RB ( \-\-password\-stdin ),
is handed to the document once, and is overwritten in memory as soon as the document
has been opened, whether or not that succeeded.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-format " txt|md"
output format (default txt)
.TP
.BI \-\-pages " <ranges>"
1-based, e.g. "1-3,7,9-" (an open end means the last page); default: every page
.TP
.BI \-\-out " <path>"
write to <path> instead of stdout (atomic: a sibling temp file, then a rename)
.TP
.BI \-\-password\-file " <path>"
the file's first line is the password
.TP
.B \-\-password\-stdin
the password is the first line of stdin
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-lines
keep the PDF's own line breaks inside a block
.TP
.B \-\-page\-marker
"--- page N ---" lines instead of a form feed between pages
.TP
.B \-\-no\-page\-breaks
no separator between pages at all
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-furniture
keep running headers, footers and page numbers
.TP
.B \-\-all\-fields
include empty text fields and unchecked boxes too
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fields
no form fields in the output, page text only
.TP
.B \-\-heuristic
ignore the document's structure tree even when present
.TP
.B \-\-strict
a page with no text layer is a failure (exit 6)
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
no informational notes on stderr (errors always print)
.TP
.B \-\-version
print the version and exit
.TP
.B \-\-help
print this and exit
.PP
Notes on a few of them:
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-pages
takes a comma-separated list of pages and ranges. Ranges that overlap or touch are
merged, and the pages come out in document order whatever order they were asked for
in. A range whose end runs past the last page is cut off at the last page; a range
whose
.I start
is past the last page is an error (exit 1), because it names a page that is not there.
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-page\-marker
in Markdown writes an HTML comment,
.BR "<!-- page N -->" ,
instead of the text marker, so it does not show in a rendered document. The default
page separator in Markdown is a blank line, not a form feed.
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-password\-file
and
.B \-\-password\-stdin
are mutually exclusive. A trailing carriage return on the line is removed, so a file
written on Windows works too.
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-out
writes the whole result to a hidden temporary file beside the destination and then
renames it into place, so a reader never sees a half-written file and an interrupted or
failed run leaves no file behind. The output is built in memory first: nothing is
written anywhere until the extraction has finished.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
text written
.TP
.B 1
usage
.TP
.B 2
cannot open
.TP
.B 3
password required or wrong
.TP
.B 4
unsupported security handler
.TP
.B 5
no text on any requested page
.TP
.B 6
\-\-strict and some requested page had no text
.TP
.B 7
\-\-out could not be written
.TP
.B 130
interrupted (Ctrl+C)
.PP
Exit code 5 is reported after the output (all placeholders) has been written, so a
script can tell "the file is a scan" from "the file could not be read". On an
interruption nothing is written to
.B \-\-out
at all.
.SH EXAMPLES
Pages 3 to 5 and page 12 of a report, as plain text, into a file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
megapdf-cli extract report.pdf \-\-pages 3\-5,12 \-\-out report.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
The whole document as Markdown, piped to another tool (here, a word count and a
pager):
.PP
.RS
.nf
megapdf-cli extract report.pdf \-\-format md | wc \-w
megapdf-cli extract report.pdf \-\-format md \-\-quiet | less
.fi
.RE
.PP
A protected document, with the password read from a file only its owner can read
(never from the command line):
.PP
.RS
.nf
(umask 077 && printf '%s\\n' 'the password' > ~/.pdf\-pass)
megapdf-cli extract protected.pdf \-\-password\-file ~/.pdf\-pass \-\-out protected.md \-\-format md
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same, from standard input, when the password is already held by another program:
.PP
.RS
.nf
some\-secret\-store show pdf\-password | megapdf-cli extract protected.pdf \-\-password\-stdin
.fi
.RE
.PP
Refuse a document that has any page without a text layer, keeping the document's own
line breaks and its headers and footers:
.PP
.RS
.nf
megapdf-cli extract scan.pdf \-\-strict \-\-keep\-lines \-\-keep\-furniture || echo "exit $?"
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/MegaPDF/megapdf-cli
the binary installed by the .deb;
.I /usr/bin/megapdf-cli
is a symbolic link to it. The tarball's
.I install.sh
installs it under
.I ~/.local/lib/megapdf/
with the link at
.IR ~/.local/bin/megapdf-cli .
.TP
.IR libmegapdf_core.so ", " libpdfium.so
the shared engine core and MegaPDF's patched PDFium, found beside the binary through
its
.B $ORIGIN
run path once the symbolic link is followed. A distribution's own libpdfium is never
loaded, on purpose: it is a different renderer, and every result MegaPDF has is
against its own.
.SH NOTES
.B megapdf-cli
ships with the MegaPDF desktop application, in the same package, and carries the same
version number (see
.BR \-\-version ).
The Markdown it writes is the same Markdown MegaPDF's own
.I Save As
writes when Markdown is chosen as the format. Text is written as UTF-8, with LF line
endings, and is never re-encoded.
.PP
Inside the Flatpak the same program runs as
.B "flatpak run \-\-command=megapdf-cli ca.electricrv.MegaPDF extract"
.IR file.pdf ,
and inside the snap as
.B "megapdf.cli extract"
.IR file.pdf .
.SH SEE ALSO
.B megapdf
.I file.pdf
opens a document in the desktop application, which has no manual page of its own.
.PP
